WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(camera clicking)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.690
- [Female Narrator] The
Secret Service is more

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:04.720
than black sunglasses, ear
pieces, and two-piece suits.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.080
- [Male Narrator] They're
also an elite team

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.680
of investigators solving some

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.690
of the nation's biggest crimes.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.790
- [Female Narrator]
Sometimes, using nothing more

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.933
than a blot of ink.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.393
Here we are: the lobby
of the Secret Service.

00:00:20.393 --> 00:00:21.650
- [Male Narrator] There were the only

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.340
authorized shots we could get.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.560
- [Female Narrator] Upstairs, in the lab,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.500
we got to see all the high-tech machines

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.810
analysts use in forensic investigations.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.910
They have names with things
like 8,000 after them

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.520
so you know they're complicated.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.820
- [Male Narrator] Did you film a printer?

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.530
- [Female Narrator] I did.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.270
- [Male Narrator] The lab
includes people like Trista,

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.350
who specializes in handwriting analysis

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.190
to investigate threat
letters or ransom notes.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.660
Jeffrey investigates
fraudulent items like IDs,

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.250
traveler's checks, credit cards.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.083
- [Female Narrator] And Irina,

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:52.170
she works in the
International Ink Library.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.710
- My name is Irina Geiman.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.210
I am a document analyst with

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.080
the United States Secret Service.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.230
The Secret Service has the
largest ink library in the world.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.813
There are over 12,000 inks

00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:04.760
that are currently in the collection.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:05.870
- [Female Narrator] This includes pens,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.913
bottled ink, and printer cartridges.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.660
- [Male Narrator] Basically,
this team of scientists

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.790
is using ink to help solve crimes.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.160
- [Female Narrator]
They're ink-vestigators.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.940
- [Male Narrator] Unh-uh,
no, don't do that.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.890
- [Female Narrator] OK,
so here's an example.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.260
Someone writes a threatening
letter to the President.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.580
- [Male Narrator] Irina
will sample that ink

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.640
and then compare it to her collection.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.430
- [Female Narrator] Exactly,
she can figure out what kind

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.520
of ink was used, and, hopefully,
it helps solve the case.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.920
Here's another ink-xample.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.590
- [Male Narrator] Nope, gimme that script.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.160
- The most counterfeited bank note

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.670
within the United States is $20,

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.830
and it's printed with inkjet, which means

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.750
somebody takes their very
simple at-home printer

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.940
and decides to print money.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.100
- [Male Narrator] Irina can
compare the counterfeit currency

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.540
to any printers found at
the scene of the crime

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.953
and help make a match.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.060
- [Female Narrator] OK,
time for a little science.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.050
- [Male Narrator] Here's
why you need 12,000 samples

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.640
of, let's be honest, mostly black ink.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.420
- [Irina] A lot of the black
inks are not just black.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.500
They're actually a collection
of different color components.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.530
There could be blues
then reds and yellows,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:09.880
and maybe a little bit of green.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.950
- [Female Narrator] Irina can,
for lack of a better term,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.330
sequence black ink like you would DNA,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.670
and find the exact breakdown of color.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.120
- [Male Narrator] That's
how she can make a match.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.500
- So what appears black at a first glance

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.900
is actually extremely
different on the inside,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.340
and it's really fun, as a chemist,

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.383
to be able to take the ink
apart and see how it's made.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.340
I'm a nerd at heart. (laughs)

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.020
I love the chemistry of it.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.330
I love the mystery of solving a case.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.090
I just really love what I do,

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.760
so it may not look
exactly like it's on TV,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.630
but it's really fun to be in an lab

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.120
and to be able to do the analysis

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.230
and to be able to help and assist all

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.940
the law enforcement agencies
that I work with daily.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:51.860
- [Female Narrator] So, Irina,

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.930
can you give us any more examples?

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.970
- It's on a need to know basis,
and you do not need to know.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:57.810
- [Male Narrator] Well, it looks like

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.556
this case is all dried up.

00:03:00.556 --> 00:03:03.139
(bright music)

